  "summary": "The coordinator's ticket-history conflict check in validate_next rejects legitimate Primary->Postcondition transitions and retries because it treats containment_id equality as a replay conflict, even though containment_id is intentionally shared across every invocation in an attempt.",
  "findings": [
    "In validate_next, the replay/conflict guard `grant.tickets.iter().any(|t| t.invocation_id == intent.invocation_id || t.containment_id == intent.containment_id || t.readiness_challenge == intent.readiness_challenge)` ORs together three fields, but containment_id is shared by design across the whole Attempt: request_attached_ticket only flips `containments.state` from 'creating' to 'live' once and every subsequent invocation (primary retries, postconditions) reuses the same job.containment_id. Once the first ticket for a Grant is recorded in grant.tickets, every later ticket request in that same Grant (e.g. the Primary->Postcondition handoff or a postcondition retry) will have a matching containment_id and be incorrectly rejected with 'ticket identity is single-use; replay the original operation' before the role/index transition table below it is ever evaluated. This makes the explicitly-supported (Primary,Primary,false), (Primary,Postcondition,true), and (Postcondition,Postcondition,*) transitions unreachable in practice. The check should only compare fields that are genuinely unique per ticket (invocation_id and/or readiness_challenge), not containment_id."
